          "question": "I have reflux and I also take ibuprofen for my knees.",
          "answer": "**Emergency signs first:** If you vomit blood or something that looks like coffee grounds, pass black, tarry stools, or feel dizzy, faint, or short of breath with any bleeding, call emergency services now. Chest pain, especially with breathlessness or sweating, also needs emergency care, because heartburn and heart pain can feel alike. (Source: Gastrointestinal bleeding guidance; Chest pain guidance)\n\n**What I could not find:** My search did not return any passage on whether ibuprofen affects reflux or how the two interact. I can't say from the reference material whether your ibuprofen is affecting your reflux, so I won't guess.\n\n**What to do next:**\n- Tell your doctor or pharmacist that you have reflux and take ibuprofen regularly. They can check whether this combination suits you.\n- Ask about other options for your knee pain, and whether a different pain reliever might be better given your reflux. Do not stop or change your ibuprofen on your own until you have spoken to them.\n- Mention any symptoms you have noticed, such as heartburn getting worse, stomach pain, or changes in your stools.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
